  n= 19: triples=3
  n= 17: triples=4
  n= 21: triples=5

Reading: the third variable turns the prime hypergraph from a thin edge
problem into a surface with enough averaging for Helfgott's theorem.

================================================================================================================
FERMAT POLYGONAL: BOUNDED ADDITIVE COVER BY POLYNOMIAL ATOMS
================================================================================================================
DP check for n <= 300; repetitions allowed; fewer than k terms allowed.
  k  max min-terms first hits max             sample representation at first hit
  3              3 [5, 8, 14, 17, 19, 23, 26, 32] [3, 1, 1]
  4              4 [7, 15, 23, 28, 31, 39, 47, 55] [4, 1, 1, 1]
  5              5 [9, 21, 31, 43, 55, 89]    [5, 1, 1, 1, 1]
  6              6 [11, 26]                   [6, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
  7              7 [13, 31]                   [7, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
  8              8 [15, 36]                   [8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]

Reading: Fermat polygonal is a uniform arity theorem.
The atom set is structured enough that a finite number of summands
beats all local obstructions.

